The input stack accepts new values. The output stack supplies old values. A transfer is done only when the output stack is empty, so repeated removals do not keep moving the same group of values.

Dry run: after `Push(4)`, `Push(7)`, and `Push(9)`, the input stack has `9` on top. The first `Pop` moves all three values, leaving `4` on top of the output stack. The next `Pop` returns `7` without another transfer.

The input and output stacks can both contain values, which is why `IsEmpty` checks both.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

class StackQueue
{
    private Stack<int> input;
    private Stack<int> output;

    public StackQueue()
    {
        input = new Stack<int>();
        output = new Stack<int>();
    }

    public void Push(int x)
    {
        input.Push(x);
    }

    public int Pop()
    {
        MoveValues();
        return output.Pop();
    }

    public int Peek()
    {
        MoveValues();
        return output.Peek();
    }

    public bool IsEmpty()
    {
        return input.Count == 0 && output.Count == 0;
    }

    private void MoveValues()
    {
        if (output.Count == 0)
        {
            while (input.Count > 0)
            {
                output.Push(input.Pop());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`Push` and `IsEmpty` are O(1). `Pop` and `Peek` are O(n) when a transfer is needed, but the amortized cost over many operations is O(1) each. Space usage is O(n).

This version makes the oldest value stay on top of `frontStack`. During `push`, the current values are moved away, the new value is placed first, and the old values are restored above it. Then `pop` and `peek` are direct operations.

The trade-off is that insertion does the extra work. It is easy to check because the first inserted value is always at the top of the active stack.

```cpp
#include <stack>

class StackQueue {
private:
    std::stack<int> frontStack;
    std::stack<int> temporaryStack;

public:
    StackQueue() {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        while (!frontStack.empty()) {
            temporaryStack.push(frontStack.top());
            frontStack.pop();
        }

        frontStack.push(x);

        while (!temporaryStack.empty()) {
            frontStack.push(temporaryStack.top());
            temporaryStack.pop();
        }
    }

    int pop() {
        int value = frontStack.top();
        frontStack.pop();
        return value;
    }

    int peek() {
        return frontStack.top();
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return frontStack.empty();
    }
};
```

Each `push` takes O(n), while `pop`, `peek`, and `isEmpty` take O(1). The two stacks use O(n) space. The platform calls `pop` and `peek` only when the queue has an element.

Here the stored list acts as the only stack. The newest item is at the end, but a queue needs the oldest item first. For `pop`, recursion removes items from the end until it reaches the bottom item. The removed items are then pushed back in the same order.

The same helper can return the bottom value without changing the queue for `peek`. This is short, but the recursive calls use extra call-stack space.

