## Intuition
A Stack follows the LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) principle, while a Queue follows FIFO (First-In, First-Out). To bridge this gap using only one stack, we must force the newest elements to the "bottom" of the stack. This ensures that the "oldest" element—the one that should be dequeued first—remains at the very top.

## Approach
We utilize the System Call Stack via recursion to temporarily hold our elements during the push operation.

- The Push Operation (Recursive):
    - If the stack is empty, we have reached the bottom. We push the new element $x$ here.
    - If not empty, we pop() the top element and store it in the current recursion's local variable val. We call push(x) again (recursively).Once the recursion returns, we push(val) back onto the stack.

- Pop/Peek Operations:Because push always places the newest element at the bottom, the "front" of our queue is always at the top of the stack.pop() and peek() simply become standard stack operations.

```Java
class StackQueue {

    Stack<Integer> stack;

    public StackQueue() {
        stack = new Stack<>();
    }

    /** Reverses the stack to put new elements at the bottom */
    public void push(int x) {
       if(stack.isEmpty()){
            stack.push(x);
            return;
       }

       // Remove top element to reach the bottom
       int val = stack.pop();
       push(x);
       // Put the element back on top of the new bottom
       stack.push(val);
    }

    public int pop() {
      return stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.pop();
    }

    public int peek() {
       return stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
}
